WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.501
-Thank you so much
for having me.

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.303
I appreciate it so much.
-We love you, buddy.

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:06.439
-Jimmy, as a magician, I believe
that nothing is random,

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.174
that every decision that
we make is based on

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.843
some decision
that was made previously,

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.145
from this moment, all the way
back to when you were born.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.446
-Okay.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:16.315
-Jimmy, I've had people in
the audience write down

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:18.751
four-digit numbers
on these cards.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:20.653
Four random -- Do you guys
remember doing that?

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:21.721
Four-random-digit numbers
on these cards.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:23.022
-Oh, really?

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:25.858
-Alright, in a second,
I'm gonna have some of The Roots

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:27.227
choose some
of these numbers, alright?

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.829
So, we're gonna start
over here with Mark.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.931
If you wouldn't mind, please
just touch one.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.867
Any one. Take it out.
Don't show anybody else.

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.504
Look at it, remember it.
Try to remember it and stand up.

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.106
-Dan, don't forget about me.
What do I do?

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:42.275
-Just touch one.
-Do I not look at you?

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.878
-Take it out, look at it,
stand up, remember it.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.813
Great. Quest,
if you wouldn't mind,

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:47.814
just touch the back of one.

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.249
-Am I dead?

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:52.919
-Good. Take it out, look at it,
remember it, stand up,

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.354
hold it against your chest.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:57.290
And, finally, Tariq, if you
wouldn't mind, just touch one.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.925
-What's happening?

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.059
This is so weird.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.728
-Good, take it out,
look at it, remember it,

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.262
hold it against your chest.

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:04.363
Alright.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:05.564
-Did you hear me at all
when I was --

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:07.333
-I didn't hear anything at all.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.402
-I was yelling at you.
-What did you say?

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.337
-Was I supposed to look?
-No, I don't want you to look.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:12.972
-Well, it's too late now.
I didn't --

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:14.774
-The numbers --
you guys remember the numbers?

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.642
Commit them to memory.
Great. Perfect. Great.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:17.644
This is where
your part comes in.

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:18.844
-Okay.

00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:20.280
-We're going to create
another random number,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.681
but this random number, we're
gonna use the audience for.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.282
I've got a ball here,
a paper ball.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.217
I want you to throw that ball
out into the audience,

00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.186
anywhere you want
at all, Jimmy, anywhere.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:30.957
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:33.493
Perfect. Great.
Miss, how are you doing?

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:37.330
I want you to think of a
number between 10 and 20.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.431
Between 10 and up to 20.
Say it now.

00:01:39.431 --> 00:01:40.933
-13.
-13.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.568
Jimmy, can you please take
one of those markers,

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:43.869
and I want you to write "13" on

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.771
those two first slots
right there.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:48.040
-Okay.
-1, 3.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.709
Great. Take that ball, throw it
somewhere else in the room,

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:50.709
anywhere you like.

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:52.712
[ Cheers and applause ]
Great.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.780
Sir, different question
this time, actually.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.617
I want you to think of
a number between 1 and 9.

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.018
Say it now.
-6.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.254
-Six. Jimmy, can you please
write down "6"?

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:03.723
Fantastic. Sir, take that ball,
throw it anywhere you want.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.425
Good, hard toss.
Very good.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:08.661
Sir, same question --
number between 1 and 9.

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:09.795
Say it out loud.
-5.

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:12.064
-5. Write down "5."
Very good.

00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:14.500
And one more toss.
Just throw it wherever you want.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.237
Great, perfect.
Same question.

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:20.272
Give me a number between
1 and 9, please, now.

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:21.407
-3.
-3.

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.743
Write down "3."
Write down "3."

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.178
Very, very good.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.747
Perfect. Great.
Okay, so, you can cap that pen.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:29.182
Let's just recap, right?

00:02:29.182 --> 00:02:32.852
We took a whole bunch of numbers
that the audience wrote down.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:35.588
The Roots then
chose four random numbers

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:36.889
from those random numbers.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:40.426
Then we threw out a ball,
making five random numbers.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.196
-Yes.
-1-3-6-5-3.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:48.434
13,653.
Did that feel random to you?

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.302
-Yes.
-That felt pretty random, right?

00:02:50.302 --> 00:02:51.905
I mean, there's no way
I could have known

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:53.306
what numbers that
they would have said.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.340
There's no way
I could have known

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.708
the numbers they wrote down.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.977
-Very seriously, I don't talk to
you before the show.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:00.046
I just whipped
that ball out there.

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.447
I didn't know who it was.
-But that's the thing.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.383
Even if I had known,
I could have no idea

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.551
who would have
gotten those numbers, right?

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.820
-Yes, random.
-Here's the interesting part.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.390
I've had a box sitting here
the entire time.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.390
It's been sitting there
the entire time.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.558
I haven't touched it.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:14.794
Inside of this box,
I brought two things.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:19.432
Two things for you.
The first thing is a present.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:21.200
I'm going to give this
to you right now.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.568
Hold on to that.
-Thank you.

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:26.839
-Great. The second thing is
a prediction of sorts.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.942
I've wrote down something before
the show even started.

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:33.179
Can you make sure that that
envelope is sealed?

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:34.347
-Yes.
-Sealed.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:36.983
Can you make sure that
this envelope is sealed?

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:38.217
-Yes, it is.
-Great.

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:41.053
Jimmy, can you please open up
the prediction.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.688
Jimmy,
would you be impressed if,

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:44.624
inside of that envelope --
open it up --

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:47.093
inside of that envelope
was the number

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:48.794
that they all
created together?

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:54.033
-Yes, I would be impressed.
Of course.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.002
-It's not. It's not, right?
It's not. It's not.

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:59.972
It's actually much more
impressive than that.

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:02.908
On this piece of paper,
I have four random numbers.

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:04.177
Four random numbers.

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.012
Now, at the beginning,
we had four random numbers

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:10.816
chosen by The Roots.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:15.254
-That's right.
-Mark, Kirk, Quest, Tariq.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:17.223
If your number is written on
this piece of paper,

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:22.629
I'd like you to sit down now.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:25.831
-What?

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:27.199
-Interesting.

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:29.601
But it gets a lot more
interesting than that,

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:32.304
because here is the crazy part.

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:39.445
If we take 8 plus 3 is 11,
plus 2, is 13.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:40.513
Carry the 1.

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:45.618
1 plus 1 plus 4
plus 4 plus 5 is 15.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.719
Carry the 1.

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:50.056
1 plus 2 plus 2
plus 5 plus 6 is 16.

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:51.090
Carry the 1.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.460
1 plus 1 plus 7 plus 2 is 13.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.296
1-3-6-5-3.

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:59.999
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:04.637
-How? How did you do that?

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:07.139
Amazing!

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:12.645
That's Dan White.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.513
-But we're not done.
We're not done.

00:05:14.513 --> 00:05:17.350
Because this isn't actually as
random as this is going to get,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.851
because we threw out
a ball earlier.

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:21.353
That ball went around the room
a bunch of different times.

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:23.556
Who was the last person
to catch that ball?

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.525
Alright, great.
Can you please stand up?

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.659
Can you please unfold that ball?

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:30.329
On that ball, I wrote something
before the show even started.

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:32.998
Can you please read --
Jimmy, can you please read out

00:05:32.998 --> 00:05:34.834
what that piece of paper says?

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:40.173
-It says, "Subtract 2,444."

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:45.844
-Subtract 2,444.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:48.281
Subtract 2,444.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.682
Now, again, remember,
at the beginning,

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.083
we created this number together.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.319
Before that,
I wrote down this number.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:58.891
Check this out.
If we take -- That is 9.

00:05:58.891 --> 00:05:59.925
Zero.

00:05:59.925 --> 00:06:01.160
-I'm so happy
I don't have to do this.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.961
-Yeah, right?

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.297
2, 1, 1.

00:06:05.297 --> 00:06:07.800
11,209.
-Yeah.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.070
-11,209.
-Yes. Okay.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.973
-We did it.
-Okay.

00:06:14.973 --> 00:06:16.975
-We did it.
-Yeah. We did it this time.

00:06:16.975 --> 00:06:19.512
-We did it.
-We really did it this time.

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.314
11,000. I get it.
-Does that feel random?

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:23.583
-Yeah, it feels --
-That feels random.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:25.618
That feels really random.
-Feels about right.

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:33.059
-Jimmy, does this number mean
anything to you, 11,209?

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:35.795
Jimmy, can you please
pull out your phone?

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.868
Jimmy, can you please
open it up?

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:46.171
And I want you to Google
the number 1-1-2-0-9.

00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:49.274
Please Google the number
1-1-2-0-9.

00:06:49.274 --> 00:06:50.709
And, at the very beginning,
I mentioned

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:52.744
that I don't believe
in randomness,

00:06:52.744 --> 00:06:55.848
that all the decisions are made
based on other decisions

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:58.417
that were made
since you were born.

00:06:58.417 --> 00:07:01.787
Jimmy, what is 11209?

00:07:01.787 --> 00:07:03.055
It's a zip code, yeah?

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:05.391
-It's a -- Yeah.
-A zip code to where?

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:09.962
-Brooklyn, New York.
-Where in Brooklyn, New York?

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:11.697
-This is Bay Ridge.
-Bay Ridge.

00:07:11.697 --> 00:07:16.802
Can you please tell everyone
here why 11209

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:18.270
is not random at all?

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.206
-I was born in Bay Ridge,
Brooklyn.

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.341
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:26.712
-And just as a souvenir,
I got you a little...

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:32.218
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:32.218 --> 00:07:35.621
-Oh, my gosh!
You are unbelievable!

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.590
Dan White! Wow!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:39.892
That was awesome!

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:44.397
